Author-Name: Luca, PENSIEROSO
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Recherches Economiques et Sociales (IRES)
Title: Real Business Cycle Models of the Great Depression : a Critical Survey
Abstract: Recent years have witnessed a revival of interest in the Great Depression of the 1930s. Among the differing new interpretations, that of the real business cycle (RBC) is particularly significant. It represents an outstanding methodological innovation in trying to cast the Great Depression within an “equilibrium” framework.  This paper critically reiews the RBC interpretation of the Great Depression, clarifying its theoretical and methodological foundations, and paving the way for future assessments of its validity. 
Classification-JEL: B22; N12
Keywords: Great Depression; Real Business Cycle Theory
Length: 42
Series: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Sciences Economiques
Number: 2005005
Creation-Date: 2005-02-01
File-URL: http://sites.uclouvain.be/econ/DP/IRES/2005-5.pdf
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:ctl:louvec:2005005

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: Vincent, BODART
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Department of Economics
Author-Name: Konstantin, KHOLODILIN
Author-Name: Fati, SHADMAN-MEHTA
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Department of Economics
Title: Identifying and Forecasting the Turning Points of the Belgian Business Cycle with Regime-Switching and Logit Models
Abstract: This paper seeks to elaborate econometric models that can be used to forecast the turning points of the Belgian business cycle.  We begin by suggesting three reference cycle, which we hope will fill the void of an official reference chronology for Belgium. We then construct two different types of model to estimate the probabilities of recession : Markov-switching models, and Logit models. We apply each approach to a limited set of data, which are a good representation of the economy, are available early and are subject to only minor revisions.  We then select the best performing model for each chronology and type of approach.  The out-of-sample results show that the models provide useful indicators of business cycle turning points.  They are however far from perfect forecasting tools, especially when it comes to forecasting periods of classical recession. 
Classification-JEL: C5; E32; E37
Keywords: Refrence chronologies; Markov-Switching and Logit models, forecasting business cycle turning points
Length: 40
Series: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Sciences Economiques
