Author-Name: Guillaume R., FRECHETTE
Author-Name: François, MANIQUET
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Department of Economics
Author-Name: Massimo, MORELLI
Title: Incumbents’ Interests, Voters’ Bias and Gender Quotas
Abstract: The adoption of gender quotas in party lists has been a voluntary decision by many parties in many countries, and is now a subject of discussion in many others.  The Parity Law passed in France in 2001 is particularly interesting because for the first time the quota was set at 50 percent, and the deputies passing the reform are elected in single member districts.  In this paper we rationalize parity on thd basis of the self interest of male incumbent deputies.  The  existence of a voters’ bias in favor of male candidates is sufficient to convince the incumbents to advocate for equal gender representation in party lists, because it raises the incumbents’ chance of being re-elected.  We confirm empirically the existence of male bias in the French electorate and we show that parity law may have Assembly composition effects and policy effects that vary with the electoral system.
Length: 48
Series: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Sciences Economiques
Number: 2006042
Creation-Date: 2006-03-01
File-URL: http://sites.uclouvain.be/econ/DP/IRES/2006-42.pdf
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:ctl:louvec:2006042

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: Olivier, BOCHET
Author-Name: François, MANIQUET
Title: Virtual Nash implementation with admissible support
Abstract: A social choice correspondence (SCC) is virtually implementable if it is ε-close (in the probability simplex) to some (exactly) implementable correspondence.  Abreu and Sen (1991) proved that, without restriction on the set of alternatives receiving strictly positive probability at equilibrium, every SCC is virtually implementable in Nash Equilibrium.  We study virtual implementation when the supports of equilibrium lotteries are restricted.  We provide a necessary and sufficient condition, imposing joint restrictions on SCCs and admissible supports.  Then, we discuss how to construct supports.  Finally, we study virtual implementation when the support is restricted to the efficient or individually rational alternatives.
Length: 23
Series: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Sciences Economiques
Number: 2006043
Creation-Date: 2006-08-01
File-URL: http://sites.uclouvain.be/econ/DP/IRES/2006-43.pdf
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:ctl:louvec:2006043

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: Jean, JASKOLD-GABSZEWICZ
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Department of Economics
Author-Name: Didier, LAUSSEL
Author-Name: Nathalie, SONNAC
Title: Advertising and competitive access pricing to internet services or pay-TV
Abstract: We study access pricing by platforms providing internet services or pay-TV to users while they allow advertisers to have access to these users against payment via ads or banners.  Users are assumed to be ad-haters.  It is shown that equilibrium access prices in the users’ market are increasing in the dimension of the advertising market : the larger the number of advertisers, the higher the access prices for both platforms.
Length: 10
Series: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Sciences Economiques
Number: 2006044
Creation-Date: 2006-09-01
File-URL: http://sites.uclouvain.be/econ/DP/IRES/2006-44.pdf
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:ctl:louvec:2006044

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: Jean J. , GABSZEWICZ
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Department of Economics
Author-Name: Didier, LAUSSEL
Author-Name: Nathalie, SONNAC
Title: Advertising and the rise of the free daily newspapers
Abstract: We analyze the competition between two newspapers in a vertical differentiation model where the qualities of the journals are determined endogenously in the first stage of the game.  We show that when the advertising revenues per reader increase there is a critical value above which the quality of the low quality newspaper discontinuously falls while it become similtaneously a free newspaper.  This is beneficial to the high quality journal and detrimental to the readers. 
Classification-JEL: L150, L820
Keywords: Advertising, Newspaper quality
Length: 13
Series: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Sciences Economiques
