Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: Anna, BATYRA
Author-Name: Henri R., SNEESSENS
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Department of Economics
Title: Selective Reductions in Labour Taxation : Labour Market Adjustments and Macroeconomic Performance
Abstract: Significant differences in unemployment in Europe have been observed across skill groups, with the least skilled suffering the highest and most persistent unemployment rates.  To identify policies alleviating this problem, we study the impact of reductions in employer social security contributions.  We construct a general equilibrium model with three types of workers and firms, matching frictions, wage bargaining and a rigid minimum wage. We find evidence in favour of narrow tax cuts targeted at the minimum wage, but we argue that it is most important to account for the effects of such reductions on both job creation and job destruction.  The failure to do so may explain the gap between macro- and microeconometric evaluations of such policies in France and Belgium.  Policy impact on welfare and inefficiencies induced by job competition, ladder effects and on-th-job search are quantified and discussed.
Classification-JEL: C68, E24, J64
Keywords: Minimum Wage, Job Creation, Job Destruction, Job Competition, Search Unemployment, Taxation, Computable General Equilibrium Models
Length: 39
Series: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Sciences Economiques
Number: 2007001
Creation-Date: 2007-02-01
File-URL: http://sites.uclouvain.be/econ/DP/IRES/2007-1.pdf
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:ctl:louvec:2007001

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: Michel, DE VROEY
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Department of Economics
Title: Marshallian and Walrasian Theory, Complementary or Alternative Approaches ? The Views in Presence
Abstract: The aim of this paper is to examine economists’ views about the relationship between Marshallian and Walrasian theory.  Are the complementary, as is usually believed, or do they constitute alternative research programmes ?  My paper compares two viewpoints on this matter, the conciliatory and the antagonistic views.  After describing these, I present my own standpoint : I do believe that there is a Marshall-Walras divide but I have serious objections to the way n which the argument for this divide is usually made.  In particular, I object to the tendency to treat the Marshallian approach as good and the Walrasian one as bad.  In the last part of the paper, I dispute the view held by several authors that an embryonic general equilibrium model is to be found in Marshall’s Principles
Length: 26
Series: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Sciences Economiques
Number: 2007002
Creation-Date: 2007-01-01
File-URL: http://sites.uclouvain.be/econ/DP/IRES/2007-2.pdf
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:ctl:louvec:2007002

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: Frederic, DOCQUIER
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Department of Economics
Author-Name: Hillel, RAPOPORT
Author-Name: I-Leng Shen
Title: Remittances and inequality : A dynamic migration model
Abstract: We develop a model to study the effects of migration and remittances on inequality in the origin communities.  While wealth inequality is shown to be monotonically reduced along the time-span, the short-and the long-run impacts on income inequality may be of opposite signs, suggesting that the dynamic relationship between migration/remittances and inequality may well be characterized by an inverse U-shaped pattern.  This is consistent with the findings of the empirical literature, yet offers a different interpretation from the usually assumed migration network effects.  With no need to endogenize migration costs through the role of migration networks, we generate the same result via intergenerational wealth accumulation
Classification-JEL: O11, O15, J61, D31
Keywords: Migrations, remittances, inequality
Length: 45
Series: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Sciences Economiques
Number: 2007003
Creation-Date: 2007-01-01
File-URL: http://sites.uclouvain.be/econ/DP/IRES/2007-3.pdf
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:ctl:louvec:2007003

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: Frederic, DOCQUIER
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Department of Economics
Author-Name: Olivier, LOHEST
Author-Name: Abdeslam, MARFOUK
Title: Brain drain in developing countries
Abstract: Relying on an original data set on international migration by educational attainment for 1990 and 2000, we analyze the determinants of the brain drain from developing countries.  We start from a simple decomposition of the brain drain in two multiplicative components, the degree of openess of sending countries (as measured by their average emigration rate) and the schooling gap (as measured by the relative education level of emigrants compared to natives).  Using various regression models, we put forward the determinants of the components and explain cross-country differences in skilled migration.  unsurprisingly, the brain drain is strong in small countries which are not too distant from the major OECD regions, which share colonial links with OECD countries and which send most of their migrants to host countries where quality-selective immigration programs exist.  More interestingly, the brain drain increases with political instability and the degree of fractionalization at origin; it globally decreases with natives’human capital.
Classification-JEL: F22, O15, J24
Keywords: International migration, Brain drain, Human capital, Developping countries
Length: 28
Series: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Sciences Economiques
Number: 2007004
Creation-Date: 2007-03-01
File-URL: http://sites.uclouvain.be/econ/DP/IRES/2007-4.pdf
