Author-Name: Raouf, BOUCEKKINE
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Department of Economics
Author-Name: Marc, GERMAIN
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Center for Operations Research and Econometrics (CORE)
Title: Impacts of emission reduction policies in a multi-regional multi-sectoral small open economy with endogenous growth
Abstract: The burden sharing of pollution abatement costs raises the issue of how to share the costs between entities (country, region or industry) and how the pollution permits should be distributed between the parties involved.  This paper explores this issue in the framework of a dynamic endogenous growth 2 sectors - 2 regions - 2 inputs Heckscher-Ohlin model of a small open multi-regional economy with an international tradable permits market.  Give an “emission-based grand-fathering” sharing rule, capital accumulation is more negatively affected by the environmental policy in the energy intensive sector.  We show that such a property does not necessarily hold with a “production-based grand-fathering” sharing rule.  We also show that the impact on capital is likely to translate into the sectoral added value level after some time, specially if the economy is submitted to an increasingly constraining environmental policy driving up the ratio price of permits to price of energy.  Finally, we show that the impact of environmental policy at the regional level depends crucially on the specialization of the region along the baseline.
Classification-JEL: D58, H21, E22, O40
Keywords: Pollution permits, Grand-fathering, Sectoral spillovers, Multi-regional economy, Endogenous growth
Length: 23
Series: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Sciences Economiques
Number: 2007010
Creation-Date: 2007-03-01
File-URL: http://sites.uclouvain.be/econ/DP/IRES/2007-10.pdf
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:ctl:louvec:2007010

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: Filippo L., CALCIANO
Title: Games with Complementarities
Abstract: We introduce a class of games with complementarities that has the quasisupermodular games, hence the supermodular games, as a special case.  Our games retain the main property of quasisupermodular games : the Nash set is a nonemply complete lattice.  We use monotonicity properties on the best reply that are weaker than those in the literature, as well as pretty simple and linked with an intuitive idea of complementarity.  The sufficient conditions on the payoffs are weaker than those in quasisupermodular games.  We also separate the conditions implying existence of a greatest and a least Nash equilibrium from those, stronger, implying that the Nash set is a complete lattice
Classification-JEL: C60, C70, C72
Keywords: Complementarity, Quasisupermodularity, Supermodular games, Monotone comparative statics, Nash equilibria
Length: 31
Series: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Sciences Economiques
Number: 2007011
