Handle: RePEc:ctl:louvec:2008015

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: Marco, MARINUCCI
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Center for Operations Research and Econometrics (CORE)
Title: Optimal ownership in joint ventures with contributions of asymmetric partners
Abstract: This paper faces two questions concerning Joint Ventures (JV) agreements.  First, we study how the partners contribution affect the creation and the profit sharing of a JV when partners’ effort is not obervable.  Then, we see whether such agreements are easier to enforce when the decision on JV profit sharing among partners is either delegated to the independent JV management (Management Sharing) or jointly taken by partners (Coordinated Sharing).  We find that the firm whose effort has a higher impact on the JV’s profits should have a larger profit shares.  Moreover, a Management sharing ensures, at least in some cases, a wider range of self-enforceable JV agreements
Classification-JEL: D43; L13; L14; L22
Keywords: Joint ventures, strategic alliances, ownership structure, asymmetries
Length: 28
Series: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Sciences Economiques
Number: 2008016
Creation-Date: 2008-06-01
File-URL: http://sites.uclouvain.be/econ/DP/IRES/2008-16.pdf
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:ctl:louvec:2008016

Template-Type: ReDIF-Paper 1.0
Author-Name: Raouf, BOUCEKKINE
Author-Workplace-Name: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Department of Economics
Author-Name: Natali, HRITONENKO
Author-Name: Yuri, YATSENKO
Title: Optimal firm behavior under environmental constraints
Abstract: The paper examines the Porter and induced-innovation hypotheses in a firm model where : (i) the firm has a vintage capital technology with two complementary factors, energy and capital; (ii) scrapping is endogenous; (iii) technological progress is energy-saving and endogenous trough purposive R&D investment; (iv) the innovation rate increases with R&D investment and decreases with complexity; (v) the firm is subject to emission quotas which put an upper bound on its energy consumption at any date; (vi) energy and capital prices are exogenous.  Balanced growth paths are first characterized, and a comparative static analysis is performed to study a kind of long-term Porter and induced-innovation hypotheses.  In particular, it is shown that tighter emission quotas do not prevent firms to grow in the long-run, thanks to endogenous innovation, but they have an inverse effect on the growth rate of profits.  Some short-term dynamics are also produced, particularly, to analyze the role of initial conditions and energy prices in optimal firm behavior subject to environmental regulation.  Among numerous results, we show that (i) firms which are historically “small” polluters find it optimal to massively pollute in the short run : during the transition, new and clean machines will co-exist with old and dirty machines in the productive sectors, implying an unambiguously dirty transition; (ii) higher energy prices induce a shorter lifetime for capital goods but they depress investment in both new capital and R&D, featuring a kind of reverse Hicksian mechanism.
Classification-JEL: C71; C78
Keywords: matching problem, von Neumann-Morgenstern stable sets, farsighted stability
Length: 52
Series: UNIVERSITE CATHOLIQUE DE LOUVAIN, Institut de Sciences Economiques
Number: 2008017
Creation-Date: 2008-06-01
File-URL: http://sites.uclouvain.be/econ/DP/IRES/2008-17.pdf
File-Format: application/pdf
Handle: RePEc:ctl:louvec:2008017

